# Pinned developer tools — single source of truth (audit 2026-09-07, T02 / PRO-02).
#
# Format: KEY=<module path>/<command>@<version>, one per line, no spaces around
# '='. That shape is valid Make syntax, valid `source` input for bash, and
# trivially parsed by PowerShell, so scripts/check.sh, scripts/check.ps1, the
# Makefile and .github/workflows/ci.yml all read THIS file instead of repeating
# the versions. Change a version here and every consumer follows.
#
# Why `go run <pkg>@<version>` rather than `go install ...@latest`:
#
#   - @latest is what broke. golangci-lint v1.64.8 was pinned, then unpinned to
#     @latest after the pin failed under go1.27 (GATE-01, ADR-0010 D3). Both
#     forms share the same flaw: the binary is built ONCE, at install time, and
#     silently stops working when the Go toolchain moves. On 2026-09-07 that had
#     already happened again to three tools at once — govulncheck, gosec and
#     staticcheck all refused to start under go1.27 with go1.26-era binaries.
#   - `go run pkg@version` rebuilds the tool with the CURRENT toolchain and
#     caches it. The version is exact, and the "binary compiled for an older Go"
#     failure mode cannot recur. First call downloads; later calls are ~1s.
#   - The go.mod `tool` directive (Go >= 1.24) was tried and REJECTED on
#     measurement: `go get -tool golangci-lint@v2.13.2` pulled the linter's
#     dependency tree into the main module, grew the module graph from 201 to
#     450 modules and, via MVS, UPGRADED a production dependency
#     (github.com/shirou/gopsutil/v4 v4.26.3 -> v4.26.7). A lint pin must not
#     move a production dependency. See docs/adr/0012 D1.
#
# Upgrading: bump the version here, run the gate, and commit the bump on its own
# so a tool change is never mixed into a code change.

# Static analysis (hard gate step 4). Config schema is v2 — a v1 binary cannot
# analyze this module.
GOLANGCI_LINT=github.com/golangci/golangci-lint/v2/cmd/golangci-lint@v2.13.2

# Vulnerability scanning (hard gate step 6, audit DEP-01 / SEC-01).
GOVULNCHECK=golang.org/x/vuln/cmd/govulncheck@v1.7.0

# Standalone security scanner. gosec also runs INSIDE golangci-lint; this pin
# exists for `make security`, which runs it with its own defaults.
GOSEC=github.com/securego/gosec/v2/cmd/gosec@v2.29.0

# Benchmark comparison (docs/PERFORMANCE.md, ADR-0009 R4 double-order protocol).
# golang.org/x/perf carries no semver tags, hence the pseudo-version.
BENCHSTAT=golang.org/x/perf/cmd/benchstat@v0.0.0-20260825160852-19be9d8e6c70
